# Scratch space for runs: installs, sandboxes, full traces.
work/
# Results that are held for disclosure. They are never committed here until
# 30 days after the maintainer was told; see README.
held/
/node_modules
# Calibration raw runs are regenerated by `node measure.ts calibrate`; only the
# pass/fail summary (calibration/result.json) is committed, as the gate proof.
calibration/runs/
# Python build byproducts from installing the local calibration package
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
calibration/liar-python/build/
# The built site. Regenerated by `node site/build.ts` from the evidence; the
# generator and the cleared list are the source, the output is not.
site/dist/
site/preview/
# Update Watch stores a full evidence file per run, two runs per version, and
# they accumulate with every version measured. The diffs are the output worth
# keeping; the runs behind them are regenerated by `node watch/cli.ts measure`.
watch/runs/
# Customer lists are not in this repository at all any more: they live in the
# private brydge-watch repo. See watch/CUSTOMERS.md. The rule stays so that a
# stray local file cannot be committed by accident.
watch/customers/
# The audit log's records. They are derived: `node watch/cli.ts backfill`
# rebuilds every one of them from watch/runs/. Kept out until there is a
# decision about publishing them, since the format does not change either way.
watch/log/
# The default approved list is state that `approve` writes, not source. It
# names what somebody signed off on and who signed it off, and this repository
# is public. The same argument as watch/CUSTOMERS.md makes about customer
# lists applies to this one. Pass --list <file> for a list kept elsewhere.
watch/approved.json
# Decisions are state that `review --apply` writes, not source. Each record
# names who signed something off and when, same argument as approved.json and
# watch/CUSTOMERS.md, and this repository is public. Pass --decisions <file>
# for a file kept elsewhere.
watch/decisions.json
# Generated review pages and the decision files exported from them. A review
# page shows the text of somebody's tool descriptions side by side, which is
# the right thing in front of a reviewer and not a thing to publish: see the
# argument in watch/tools.ts about watch/runs. Regenerated by
# `node watch/cli.ts review --page <pkg> <a> <b>`, and never built into
# site/dist, which test/site-no-descriptions.test.ts checks.
watch/review/
# The gate journal and the room runs it leaves behind. Derived state: a
# journal names which tools a gate would have hidden and when, which is a
# record of somebody's running setup, and the same argument as
# watch/decisions.json applies.
watch/gate/journal/
# A durable copy of the recorded session transcripts, for replaying the gate
# against real servers. The sandboxes they came from are deleted once their
# evidence is stored, so the corpus they are taken from is not kept; this is.
# Every message a server sent is in them, descriptions included, which is the
# same reason watch/runs is ignored.
watch/transcripts/
# The gate build. Derived from the source beside it and never committed: a
# built artefact in the repository drifts from its source and nothing
# notices. Rebuilt by "node watch/gate/build.mjs"; the gate refuses to run
# from a build older than its sources.
watch/gate/built/
# A pin carries the tool definitions it pinned, so that a later difference
# can be shown against them. That makes it a file of somebody else's prose,
# which is the argument watch/tools.ts makes about watch/runs.
watch/gate/pins/
# Reports the gate writes when a live tool list differs from its pin. Same
# reason: the diff is of descriptions.
watch/gate/pending/
